/* ---------- v3: practical cases (midterm site, put in the course method, + end-term) ---------- */
const FACTS={p1:'Two clients want to register non-traditional signs: a single colour (pink), a sound (a cow’s moo filed as a sonogram), a position sign (an orange strip on the toe of a sock) and a shape (an egg, for cosmetics).',
 p2:'Four signs filed for given goods: the words “FreshMilk” (pasteurised milk), a blue sole (trainers), “SugarFree” (sweets containing sugar), “LuxuryBag” (handbags).',
 p3:'A cosmetics company considers seven signs for perfumery, body and face care and make-up: two word signs (“Beauty Cares”, “Beautiful”), a colour, a motion sign, a sound, and two word signs at risk of deceiving (“Cannabis”, “Milky care”).',
 p4:'A drop-shipper wants to sell phone and AirPods cases bearing third parties’ registered (and reputed) trademarks: Coca-Cola, Starbucks, Disney characters, without authorisation.',
 p6:'A company with a company name, a commercial name (shop) and a domain name wants to register six signs for construction machinery (a word, descriptive words, a colour, a sound, a slogan, a functional shape); a third party uses a similar domain name for garden furniture.',
 p7:'A Lyon start-up wants to register six signs for chocolates: a word sign with a false geographical origin, a logo with a national flag, a smell, a shape giving value, a multimedia sign and a pattern.',
 p8:'Three uses of other people’s marks in trade: a keyword bought on a search engine (Lacoste), a perfume name close to a famous one (Chanel), merchandise with a football club’s crest and a “not official” sign.'};
const toMethod=(id,m)=>FACTS[id]?m.replace('<div class="irac">',`<div class="irac">\n<b>1. Classification of facts</b><div>${FACTS[id]}</div>`).replace('<b>Issue</b>','<b>2. Legal question</b>').replace('<b>Rule</b>','<b>3. Rule(s) of law</b>').replace('<b>Application</b>','<b>4. Application</b>').replace('<b>Conclusion</b>','<b>5. Conclusion</b>'):m;
const SHORT_MODEL={p1:'x-t3-clients',p3:'x-t4-sarah'};
const OLDCASE=id=>{const x=EXERCISES.find(e=>e.id===id);return x?x.solution:''};
const PRACT=MIDPRACT.map(p=>({id:p.id,topic:CH2T[p.ch],src:'Midterm site · '+p.src,title:p.title,facts:p.facts,tasks:p.tasks,hints:p.hints,
  model:toMethod(p.id,p.model)+(SHORT_MODEL[p.id]?`<h4>Short version (common slides, Sessions 3–4)</h4>${OLDCASE(SHORT_MODEL[p.id])}`:'')})).concat([
 {id:'e-sandra',topic:'t5',src:'Session 5 · case study (slide 21)',title:'Sandra’s brand “My Cave of Wonders”',
  facts:'<p>Sandra wants to start a business selling <b>furniture</b> as well as <b>audio and video devices</b> under the brand below (the words “MY CAVE OF WONDERS”, “OF” above a dark pyramid, three yellow stars above).</p><p style="font:700 1.05rem var(--f-display);letter-spacing:.12em;text-align:center">☆<br>☆ &nbsp; OF &nbsp; ☆<br>MY CAVE ▲ WONDERS</p><p>She is about to open 2 shops in Paris and will soon expand her activity across the European Union and the US. She asks whether this brand can be registered as a trademark.</p>',
  tasks:['Qualify the facts: which sign, which goods (Nice classes), which territories?','Is the sign valid (absolute grounds)?','Is it available (relative grounds: earlier marks, other prior rights)? Where do you search?','Which filing route and which fees?'],
  hints:['A complex mark: word elements + figurative elements. Check distinctiveness for classes 20 (furniture) and 9 (audio/video devices).','Relative grounds: Art. 5 (three cases), other prior rights (company names, domain names, copyright on the drawing). Search TMview, DesignView, Infogreffe, WHOIS.','Territory: France now, EU soon, US later: EU trademark + Madrid within 6 months.'],
  model:`<div class="irac">
<b>1. Classification of facts</b><div>Sandra wants to register a <b>complex sign</b> (word elements “MY CAVE OF WONDERS” + figurative elements: stars, pyramid) as a trademark for <b>furniture</b> (Nice class 20) and <b>audio and video devices</b> (class 9), first in France, then in the EU and the US.</div>
<b>2. Legal question</b><div>Can this sign be registered, i.e. is it <b>valid</b> (absolute grounds) and <b>available</b> (relative grounds) in the territories concerned, and how should she file?</div>
<b>3. Rule(s) of law</b><div><p>Art. 3 Directive 2015/2436 (a sign, distinctive, represented clearly and precisely); Art. 4 (devoid, descriptive, customary, deceptive, public policy…). Art. 5 (relative grounds): identical sign and goods + adverse effect on a function; identical or similar sign and goods + likelihood of confusion (Sabel, Canon, Lloyd); reputed mark, even for dissimilar goods. Other prior rights: copyright, designs, GIs; company names, commercial names, domain names (unfair competition). Territoriality: national marks, EU trademark (27 states), Madrid System; priority 6 months (Art. 4 Paris Convention).</p></div>
<b>4. Application</b><div><p><b>Validity</b>: a figurative mark is filed as a reproduction of the image. “My Cave of Wonders” neither designates nor describes furniture or audio/video devices: it is evocative at most (a place full of wonderful things), so <b>distinctive</b>; the stars and pyramid add figurative distinctiveness. Nothing deceptive or contrary to public policy.</p>
<p><b>Availability</b>: search <b>TMview</b> for identical or similar earlier marks (e.g. “Cave of Wonders”, “Cave of Marvels”) in classes 9 and 20 and related goods (furniture vs home decoration; audio devices vs electronics), in France, the EU and the US; compare the signs visually, aurally and conceptually with the dominant element “CAVE OF WONDERS”. Check also <b>reputed</b> marks, which are protected even for other goods (an expression known from a famous film, for instance, could be part of a reputed mark: check the register before filing). Then company names (Infogreffe), domain names (WHOIS) and copyright on the drawing (Sandra must own the rights on the logo: assignment from the designer).</p></div>
<b>5. Conclusion</b><div>If no conflicting prior right is found, the sign is valid and available: file an <b>EU trademark</b> (covers France and the EU expansion: €850 + €50 for 2 classes = <b>€900</b>), then use the <b>Madrid System</b> for the US within <b>6 months</b> to keep the priority date. If an earlier similar mark exists for similar goods, change the sign or negotiate a coexistence before investing in the shops.</div></div>
<h4>Short version (previous model of the site)</h4>${OLDCASE('x-t5-sandra')}`+NOTE('<b>Model by the site:</b> the slides give no solution. The search results (whether an earlier “Cave of Wonders” mark exists) cannot be known without running the search.')},
 {id:'e-champollion',topic:'t6',src:'Session 6 · case study (slide 19)',title:'Champollion: one trademark per activity, in different countries',
  facts:'<p>The company CHAMPOLLION has developed different sectors of activity throughout the world. They need your advice to register one trademark for each activity, in different countries. They need to protect:</p><ul><li><b>TM1</b> in Germany, Poland, Austria, Hungary and the Czech Republic;</li><li><b>TM2</b> in France, Belgium, Switzerland, Canada;</li><li><b>TM3</b> in Germany, Poland, Austria, Hungary, the Czech Republic, Japan and China… and they would like to enter the UK and US markets in the next 4 years.</li></ul>',
  tasks:['For each mark, which countries are EU Member States?','Which route: national filings, EU trademark, Madrid System? In which order?','How does the priority right help, and what is its limit for TM3?'],
  hints:['Territoriality: one office, one territory; the EU trademark covers the 27.','Madrid needs a first (basic) mark; the priority right gives 6 months.'],
  model:`<div class="irac">
<b>1. Classification of facts</b><div>A company wants three trademarks, each protected in a list of countries inside and outside the EU, now and (for TM3) in the next 4 years.</div>
<b>2. Legal question</b><div>Which registration routes give protection in each list of countries, at the best cost and with the earliest date?</div>
<b>3. Rule(s) of law</b><div>Territoriality: one registration protects in one territory; the EU trademark (EUIPO) covers the 27 Member States. Madrid System (Protocol 1989, 131 countries): from a first mark, one form designating several countries, a bundle of national rights. Priority right (Art. 4 Paris Convention): 6 months to file abroad with the same date.</div>
<b>4. Application</b><div><p><b>TM1</b>: Germany, Poland, Austria, Hungary and Czechia are all EU Member States → one <b>EU trademark</b> (from €850), simpler and cheaper than five national filings.</p>
<p><b>TM2</b>: France and Belgium are in the EU; Switzerland and Canada are not → a first filing (French mark at INPI, or an EU mark), then the <b>Madrid System</b> designating the other countries (Benelux, Switzerland, Canada if the first mark is French), within 6 months to keep the priority date.</p>
<p><b>TM3</b>: EU trademark for the five EU countries + Madrid designating Japan and China within 6 months (priority). The UK and the US can be added later (subsequent designation), but after 6 months <b>without priority</b>: a third party could file first.</p></div>
<b>5. Conclusion</b><div>TM1: EU trademark. TM2: first mark + Madrid within 6 months. TM3: EU trademark + Madrid now; file for the UK and the US as early as possible (ideally within the 6-month priority period) if the plan is firm.</div></div>
<h4>Short version (previous model of the site)</h4>${OLDCASE('x-t6-champo')}`+NOTE('<b>Model by the site.</b> <b>Outside the slides:</b> Belgium is designated through the Benelux office; the UK left the EU in 2020 and is not covered by an EU trademark; “subsequent designation” is a Madrid mechanism not described in the slides.')},
 {id:'e-lumio',topic:'t5',src:'New scenario (site)',title:'LUMIO vs LUMIA: is the sign available?',
  facts:'<p>A start-up wants to register the word “LUMIO” as an EU trademark for <b>smart lamps</b> and <b>home-lighting installation services</b>. A search on TMview shows an earlier EU mark “LUMIA”, registered 12 years ago for <b>lamps and light bulbs</b>, used across the EU with a large advertising budget, but not known outside the lighting sector.</p>',
  tasks:['Which case of Art. 5 applies?','Run the global assessment factor by factor.','What will the owner of LUMIA do, and when?'],
  hints:['Signs: identical or similar? Goods: identical, similar, complementary?','Five factors (Sabel, Canon, Lloyd), interdependent.','Opposition: within 3 months of publication at EUIPO.'],
  model:`<div class="irac">
<b>1. Classification of facts</b><div>An EU application for a word sign close to an earlier EU word mark, for goods identical (lamps) and services related (installation of lighting).</div>
<b>2. Legal question</b><div>Is “LUMIO” available, or is there a likelihood of confusion with the earlier mark “LUMIA” (Art. 5(1)(b))?</div>
<b>3. Rule(s) of law</b><div>Art. 5(1)(b) Directive: identical or similar sign, identical or similar goods, likelihood of confusion including association. Canon: risk that the public believes the goods come from the same or economically linked undertakings. Global assessment of interdependent factors (Sabel, Canon): similarity of signs (visual, aural, conceptual; dominant elements), similarity of goods (nature, users, use, competition, complementarity; not the Nice class), distinctiveness of the earlier mark, relevant public with an imperfect recollection (Lloyd).</div>
<b>4. Application</b><div><p><b>Signs</b>: LUMIO / LUMIA share four of five letters and the beginning; aurally very close; conceptually both evoke light (“lum-”). Similar to a high degree. <b>Goods</b>: smart lamps and lamps are identical or highly similar; installation services are complementary to lamps (same users, same channel). <b>Earlier mark</b>: long, wide use and a large budget increase its distinctiveness, although “lum-” is slightly evocative. <b>Public</b>: the average consumer of lamps, normal attention, imperfect recollection. Overall: likelihood of confusion. Reputation is not needed here (the goods are similar) and is not shown outside the sector.</p></div>
<b>5. Conclusion</b><div>“LUMIO” is not available for lamps. LUMIA’s owner will file an <b>opposition</b> within <b>3 months</b> of the publication of the application (or later ask for cancellation, and sue for infringement if LUMIO is used). Advice: choose another sign.</div></div>`+NOTE('<b>Scenario and model written by the site</b> on the rules of Session 5.')},
 {id:'e-korva',topic:'t7',src:'New scenario (site)',title:'KORVA: counterfeits, parallel goods and a licensee',
  facts:'<p>KORVA SAS owns the French trademark “KORVA” for sneakers. It discovers that: (1) a Lille shop sells fake KORVA sneakers; (2) another shop sells genuine KORVA sneakers bought from KORVA’s German distributor; (3) its exclusive licensee in Belgium keeps selling after the licence expired; (4) a garage puts up the sign “We customise KORVA sneakers”.</p>',
  tasks:['For each situation: infringement or not? Which rule or exception?','Which steps to gather evidence and which court?','Which remedies can KORVA obtain?'],
  hints:['Three cases of infringement; exceptions: honest use, exhaustion.','Saisie-contrefaçon, 15 days, tribunal judiciaire of Lille; civil and criminal remedies.'],
  model:`<div class="irac">
<b>1. Classification of facts</b><div>A French registered mark used by others in four ways: fakes, resale of genuine goods first sold in Germany, use by a former licensee, a reference to the mark by a service provider.</div>
<b>2. Legal question</b><div>Which of these uses are trademark infringement, and how can KORVA act?</div>
<b>3. Rule(s) of law</b><div>Infringement: use in the course of trade in one of the three cases (double identity + adverse effect on a function; confusion; reputed mark). Exceptions: honest use (descriptive or referential use), exhaustion (goods put on the EEA market by the owner or with consent, unless legitimate reasons such as alteration). Licence: use within the limits of the contract (duration). Procedure: saisie-contrefaçon (L.716-4-7 IPC; sue within 15 days), right of information; 10 specialised tribunaux judiciaires (Lille) or tribunal correctionnel. Remedies: injunction, destruction, damages (no punitive damages), publication; criminal: up to €400 000 and 4 years.</div>
<b>4. Application</b><div><p>(1) Fakes: identical sign, identical goods, the origin function is affected → <b>infringement</b>. (2) Genuine sneakers sold in Germany by KORVA’s distributor: put on the EEA market with consent → <b>exhaustion</b>, no infringement (unless the sneakers were altered). (3) Former licensee: after the end of the licence, it has no authorisation; selling under the mark is use in the course of trade → <b>infringement</b> (and breach of contract). (4) “We customise KORVA sneakers”: <b>honest use</b> to indicate the purpose of the service, if it does not suggest an official link; but altering the sneakers may give KORVA a legitimate reason to oppose their further commercialisation.</p>
<p>Evidence: saisie-contrefaçon at the Lille shop (description and seizure), then sue within 15 days; right of information on the supplier of the fakes; customs watch.</p></div>
<b>5. Conclusion</b><div>KORVA can sue the Lille shop and the former licensee before the tribunal judiciaire of Lille (injunction, destruction, damages, publication) and file a criminal complaint against the counterfeiter; it cannot stop the resale of genuine German sneakers nor the honest reference by the garage.</div></div>`+NOTE('<b>Scenario and model written by the site</b> on the rules of Session 8.')}]);

